counterfactuals.output:
The invention provides a highly concentrated free-fatty-acid composition in which at least 80 wt% of the fatty acids are C20-or-higher polyunsaturated fatty acids, such as EPA, DHA, or related long-chain PUFAs. Unlike conventional fish-oil concentrates, the fatty acids are predominantly in the free-acid form and have very low levels of contaminating metals, making the composition suitable for pharmaceutical, nutritional, and other oxidation-sensitive applications.

The process generally converts a PUFA-rich ester or glyceride feed into free fatty acids by hydrolysis or saponification, followed by acidification, phase separation, washing, and purification. Metal pickup is controlled mainly during the acidic stages, when ordinary stainless-steel equipment is most susceptible to corrosion. The important controls are:

- carry out acidification and subsequent handling at a relatively low temperature;
- minimize the time that the strongly acidic fatty-acid mixture contacts metal surfaces;
- avoid excessive acidity and add the acid in a controlled manner;
- use corrosion-resistant, coated, glass-lined, resin-lined, or otherwise nonmetallic wetted surfaces for acidification, separation, and storage;
- promptly separate and wash the liberated fatty-acid phase to remove residual acid, alkali-metal salts, and dissolved metals; and
- conduct final concentration or distillation under reduced pressure, preferably under an inert atmosphere, to limit both thermal oxidation and further equipment corrosion.

Together, these conditions yield an 80%-plus long-chain PUFA free-fatty-acid concentrate while keeping iron and other process-derived metals at very low levels.
